# 5‐Chloro‐2‐Hydroxypyridine Derivatives with Push‐Pull Electron Structure Enable Durable and Efficient Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Targeted passivation of defects in perovskite is the primary consideration in the design of additives containing functional groups. However, the precise modulation of electron structure in functional groups and the structure‐activity relationship between electronic configuration and performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) still need to be explored. In this study, 5‐chloro‐2‐hydroxypyridine derivatives with –NH2 (HNCLP) and –F (HFCLP) end groups are selected to realize the push‐pull electronic structure configuration. Density functional theory demonstrates that, compared with HFCLP, HNCLP with the electron‐donating terminal of –NH2 has a long dipole moment and immobilize the interstitial I3−, and the N side of pyridine with high‐density electron cloud enables strong passivation with undercoordinated Pb2+ ions. The experimental results confirm that HNCLP with optimized electronic configuration emerges strong passivation ability, greatly suppresses the nonradiative recombination of perovskite absorber, and remarkably improves the film crystal quality along with the extraction and transfer process of photogenerated carriers. The HNCLP‐contained PSC exhibits a remarkable efficiency of 24.47%, and HNCLP helps to enhance the moisture‐proof of perovskite film and device storage stability.

Targeted passivation of defects in perovskite is the primary consideration in the design of additives containing functional groups. However, the precise modulation of electron structure in functional groups and the structure-activity relationship between electronic configuration and performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) still need to be explored. In this study, 5-chloro-2- hydroxypyridine derivatives with $\left|-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ (HNCLP) and $-F$ (HFCLP) end groups are selected to realize the push-pull electronic structure configuration. Density functional theory demonstrates that, compared with HFCLP, HNCLP with the electron-donating terminal of – $-N H_{2}$ has a long dipole moment and immobilize the interstitial ${\mathsf{I}}_{3}^{\mathrm{~-~}}$ , and the N side of pyridine with high-density electron cloud enables strong passivation with undercoordinated $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ ions. The experimental results confirm that HNCLP with optimized electronic configuration emerges strong passivation ability, greatly suppresses the nonradiative recombination of perovskite absorber, and remarkably improves the film crystal quality along with the extraction and transfer process of photogenerated carriers. The HNCLPcontained PSC exhibits a remarkable efficiency of $24.47\%$ , and HNCLP helps to enhance the moisture-proof of perovskite film and device storage stability.
## 1. Introduction
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite, one of the frontrunners in photovoltaic field in recent years, has well-known advantages, including tunable band gap, excellent carrier mobility, long-range carrier diffusion, and low-energy exciton binding, which have enabled its efficiency to grow rapidly to $26.0\%$ , making it a strong contender for next-generation cells.[1–5] Moreover, due to its simple and low-cost manufacturing process, it has received widespread attention. However, the record-breaking power conversion efficiency (PCE) obtained so far has not yet reached the Shockley–Queisser limit efficiency, and there is an urgent need to optimize the perovskite films to reduce the nonradiative recombination loss, thereby improving the performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs).[6–10]
After extensive research, it is found that $\mathrm{FA}_{1-X}\mathrm{MA}_{X}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ -based PSCs have received widespread attentions (Table S1, Supporting Information). However, because of the thermal annealing process of perovskite, multiple defects are easily formed at its interface and internal grain boundaries, which impede charge carrier transport and act as a channel for ion migration, accelerate moisture penetration and severely degrade the perovskite absorber. Therefore, the perovskite absorber with low defect density is one of the prerequisites for determining high-performance PSCs.[11–17] Additives containing a variety of organic functional groups can achieve targeted passivation of perovskite defects, including amines, carboxylates, basic and acidic functional groups, as well as some aliphatic chains and hydrophobic groups, effectively reducing water penetration into perovskite films.[18–29] Several additives containing Lewis acid/base groups are recognized particularly effective in passivating perovskite grain boundaries, where they attach to the unliganded ions to inhibit ion migration.[30–32]
Functional group anchoring has been widely investigated as a common approach in the photovoltaic field.[33–35] Liang et al. adopted phenethylammonium iodide and diethylenediamine iodide to purposely inactivate $\mathrm{{Pb}}$ - and Sn-based defect centers, respectively, which can target the solidify dual-source defects in perovskite materials. This effectively enhances the directional growth quality of perovskite crystals and also improves carrier transport.[36] Chen et al. used pyridine functionalized poly(triarylamine) for inverted PSCs, which not only possess excellent photovoltaic performance, but also exhibit good photothermal stability.[37] Xie et al. designed a green anti-solvent additive to enhance the electron-withdrawing ability of Cl group, thereby making the preparation process more environmentally friendly and economical.[38] Li et al. selected $\mathsf{S}{=}\mathsf{C}{-}\mathsf{N}$ functional groups to enhance electron density distribution and diminish the defect density of deep energy level trap states caused by $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ ion undercoordination and $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ oxidation of $\mathrm{CsSNi}_{3}$ perovskite.[39] Noticeably, pyridine derivatives are more suitable for application in the perovskite photovoltaic field in conjunction with other functional groups. However, there are few study on the combination of pyridine derivatives with electron-withdrawing and electron-donating functional groups to anchor and passivate perovskite defects. It is well-known that the functional group F has strong electron-withdrawing function, while the functional group $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ has strong electron-donating ability.
Inspired by this, on the basis of 5-chloro-2-hydroxypyridine derivatives with $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (HNCLP) and –F (HFCLP) end groups, we propose two molecules with push-pull electronic structure configuration. The regulatory effect of electron density distribution on the dipole moment of derivatives is subsequently investigated. The correlation between the electronic structure of molecules and defect passivation in perovskite and the structure-activity of devices has been explored. Simulation calculation indicates that HNCLP with a push-electron structure can aggregate the electron density and tend to combine with interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ , promote the aggregation of negative electron density at the N-terminal of pyridine and more easily bind to the undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , suggesting that HNCLP efficiently passivates defects, improves the quality of perovskite film, and expands carrier transport. Because of the effectiveness of the push-electron structure HNCLP, the device efficiency has been improved from $22.27\%$ in the control group to $24.47\%$ , accompanied by significant increases in opencircuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ and fill factor (FF). Moreover, the HNCLP molecule makes the perovskite film have excellent hydrophobicity and the device have air-storage stability.
## 2. Results and Discussion
5-Chloro-2-hydroxypyridine is commonly used as an additive for the prevention and treatment of coccidiosis in livestock and poultry, and is widely adopted in the pharmaceutical field. Due to its special functional groups, it has the potential to enhance the crystal quality of perovskite absorber. Among them, pyridine is often designed to bind undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions and inhibits the nonradiative recombination in perovskite. Moreover, the leakage of heavy metal lead ions in PSCs inevitably damages the environment and causes harm to the human body. Therefore, passivating undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions can also indirectly suppress lead leakage issues.[40–43] what is more, numerous studies have reported that interstitial iodine ions are prone to causing phase transitions in perovskites, disrupting the phase stability of films, and hindering the stability of devices. [44–46] The hydroxyl group (–OH) has been proved to have cross-linking effect, which can form hydrogen bonds with the $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ ions and inhibit ion migration.[47,48] Previous study demonstrated that the Cl-treated perovskite films exhibited high crystallinity with low-density trap states.[49,50] Therefore, 5-chloro-2-hydroxypyridine derivatives are adopted to passivate undercoordinated lead ions and interstitial iodine ions for durable and efficient PSCs. Generally, because of the post-annealing process of preparing perovskite absorber, iodine atoms are prone to abandon the regular position in the crystal lattice, forming iodine vacancy defects and leading a large number of undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . The imperfect iodine atoms will occupy the vacancy between the lattices and form interstitial iodine, which generates nonradiative recombination sites.[51–53] Based on multifunctional 5-chloro-2-hydroxypyridine molecule, two derivatives with push-pull electron structure are proposed, which combines the typical strong electron-withdrawing group of F and the strong electron-donating group of $\mathsf{\bar{\Pi}}_{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ . Two push-pull electronic functional groups are substituted at the same position of 5-chloro-2-hydroxypyridine to modify undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ . For simplicity, 5-chloro-2-hydroxypyridine derivatives with –F and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups are represented as HFCLP and HNCLP, respectively.
The electrostatic potential (ESP) distributions of HFCLP and HNCLP indicate that the hydrogen on the pyridine ring is replaced by F as a strong absorbing electron (Figure 1a), reducing the electron cloud density, while the C─F bond length is short and the high electron density region is pulled to both sides. In contrast, the hydrogen at the identical position is occupied by $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ as a strong electron donor, increasing the electron cloud density and pushing the high electron density region back together. Moreover, the aggregated positive potential surface is more electrophile and is easy to combine with the interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ , and also allows the negative electron density region to gather on pyridine N, making it prone to bind to undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions. The redistribution of electron density contributes to a dipole moment of 4.46 Debye for HNCLP (Figure 1b), which is much larger than that of HFCLP (2.05 Debye). Suitable dipole molecule helps improve the passivation effect of defects, which has been verified in the subsequent experiments. The binding energy of HFCLP and HNCLP with the undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ is obtained by density functional theory (DFT) (Figure 1c–f), and theoretical models of regional amplification are shown in Figure S1, Supporting Information. Since pyridine $\mathrm{~N~}$ in HNCLP has a strong electron-donating functional group $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , the negative electron density region aggregated on pyridine N makes it easier to passivate the undercoordination $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , so that its binding energy $(-2.28~\mathrm{eV})$ is greater than that of HFCLP $(-2.01\ \mathrm{eV})$ . The high electron density region accumulated on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in HNCLP makes the binding energy $(-1.89\ \mathrm{eV})$ with interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ better than that of HFCLP $(-0.15\mathrm{eV})$ , implying that HNCLP has a good fixing effect on interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ . On the basis of above calculations, it is better clarified that the push-pull electron functional group causes the redistribution of electron density and the efficient passivation effect of the undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ .
The nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra are measured to explore the interaction between $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and two molecules of HNCLP and HFCLP. The HNCLP and HFCLP molecules and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ are dissolved in deuterated DMSO in the form of 1:1, respectively. The $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra (Figure S2, Supporting Information) indicate that after the reaction of the two molecules with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , no other peaks appear except for the peaks of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and their respective molecular structure. The results suggest that no new products are formed after the interaction, but the characteristic peaks of the two molecules shift, implying that both molecules are bonded to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the form of hydrogen bonds. Figure 2a shows the amplified $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR hydrogen spectra of pure HNCLP and its reaction with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the range of $5.40{-}5.52~\mathrm{ppm}$ . The peak represents the active hydrogen of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ on HNCLP, which undergoes a shift after reacting with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , suggesting that the electron-donating functional group $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ can bind to iodide ions. The $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ NMR spectra of pure HFCLP and its reaction with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ are exhibited (Figure 2b), and the change of the peak also reveals that the electron-withdrawing group F is bonded to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . For the functional group –OH on the two molecules, the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra present that the peak of active hydrogen on the $-\mathrm{OH}$ of HNCLP shifts to a high field and the chemical shift value decreases, while the peak of active hydrogen on the $-\mathrm{OH}$ of HFCLP moves to a low field and the chemical shift value increases (Figure 2c). This is because $-\mathrm{F}$ , as a strong withdrawing electron substituent, replaces hydrogen on the pyridine ring, which reduces the electron cloud density and produces a deshielding effect, while $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , as a strong donating electron moiety, occupies hydrogen at the identical position on the pyridine ring, which increases the electron cloud density and produces a shielding effect.[54] Meanwhile, it is found that after the two molecules react with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , although the shift directions of the active hydrogen on the –OH of the two molecules are different, the shift degrees of the peaks are the same. Although –F substituent on HFCLP pulls the high electron region to both sides, and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ moiety on HNCLP has a higher cluster electron density region than the $-\mathrm{OH}$ side, the degree of interaction between –OH on the two molecules and $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ ion is almost the same. The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of pure HFCLP and pure HNCLP and their reactions with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the full spectrum (Figure S3, Supporting Information) and $1470{-}1640\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ range (Figure 2d) are shown, respectively. The concentration ratio of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ to the two additives is adjusted to 1:1 to clearly observe their interaction. After reacting with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , the peak position of the $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ bond of HFCLP and HNCLP changes from 1632.16 to 1565.90 and from 1631.36 to $1557.17~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The larger peak shift indicates a closer interaction between HNCLP and the undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , which is more effective in reducing $\mathrm{Pb}$ -related defects.[55] The coordination interaction between two molecules with push-pull electron structure and perovskite is explored through X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurement (Figure 2e-g). In general, during the preparation of perovskite film, Pb-related defects negatively affect the performance and lifetime of the devices. In Figure 2e, two peaks of Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ in the as-prepared perovskite can be clearly observed at 143.06 and $138.32\ \mathrm{eV}_{\mathrm{:}}$ respectively. When HFCLP molecule is added, the Pb 4f peaks shift along the high binding energy direction. For the perovskite containing HNCLP, the binding energy further shifts to 143.38 and $138.50\ \mathrm{eV}$ toward a higher level, which is mainly due to the higher density of the negative electron region of N on the pyridine ring of HNCLP than that of HFCLP, decreasing the electron density around Pb after binding with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . This also indicates that HNCLP has a stronger binding ability to $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions, which is in line with the ESP results.[56] The peak of N 1s in the control perovskite shift from $400.66~\mathrm{eV}$ to high binding energy to 400.71 and $400.79\ \mathrm{eV}$ for HFCLP- and HNCLP-treated samples (Figure 2f), respectively, indicating a strong binding ability between HNCLP and A-site organic cations. The two I 3d peaks exhibit the similar changes as the Pb 4f peaks (Figure 2g), that is, the introduction of HNCLP causes the I 3d peaks to move to the maximum range in the direction of high binding energy, suggesting that $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ is more conducive to passivating interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ .[57] The signal peak of Cl $2\mathrm{p}$ do not appear in the control perovskite (Figure S4, Supporting Information), but it has been detected in both molecule-modified films, confirming that the two molecules are indeed successfully introduced into the film. The introduction of Cl may improve the densification of the perovskite absorber.
The UV–vis absorption spectra of the perovskite films without and with HFCLP and HNCLP treatments (Figure S5, Supporting Information) presents little change in absorption intensity and optical band gap. Combined with the ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements (Figure S6, Supporting Information), the Fermi energy levels and energy band alignment of all perovskite-related films are calculated (Figure 3a). The Fermi energy levels are $-4.28$ , $-4.62$ , and $-4.63\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the asfabricated, HFCLP-, and HNCLP-modified perovskites, respectively. The downward moving energy level structure significantly advances the extraction and transport of photogenerated electrons, which is conducive to the enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ .[58,59] By analyzing Urbach energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{u}}\right)$ , the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss is further explored (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The $E_{\mathrm{u}}$ value reflects the sub-bandgap absorption typically caused by shallow level defects. After calculation and experimental analysis, HNCLP molecule effectively passivates two shallow level defects, interstitial ${\mathrm{I}_{3}}^{-}$ ion and undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ion. Therefore, the HNCLP-modified film has a low $E_{\mathrm{u}}$ value, suggesting that it has a large diffusion length, long carrier lifetime, and small $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss.
After a series of chemical theoretical calculations and experimental analysis, it has been proved that two molecules with push-pull electron structure indeed passivate the defects. The photocurrent density-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves for the cells with the control and HFCLP/HNCLP-treated absorbers are presented in Figure 3b, and the specific photovoltaic parameters are exhibited in the inset and (Figure S8, Supporting Information). When measuring the $J{-}V$ curves, the additional mask is not introduced to fix the aperture area. The preparation area of $0.3\ \mathrm{cm}$ $\times0.3~\mathrm{cm}$ is the pixel area and measurement area of the device. The optimal PCE of the pristine cell is $22.27\%$ along with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of 1.118 V, short-circuit current density $(J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}})$ of $25.16~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and fill factor (FF) of $79.12\%$ . After HFCLP treatment, all photovoltaic parameters are enhanced, and the highest PCE is $23.18\%$ However, after HNCLP treatment, the photovoltaic characteristics of the cell, especially $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF, have been significantly enhanced, achieving best efficiency of up to $24.47\%$ as well as $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.172\mathrm{~V},J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $25.24~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $82.70\%$ which demonstrating that the aggregated high-density electrons in HNCLP are beneficial for binding and passivating the undercoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and interstitial $\mathrm{I}^{3-}$ related defects, lowering the nonradiative recombination, and enhancing cell performance. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra confirm that the photocurrent density of the as-fabricated, HFCLP-, and HNCLP-contained PSCs are 25.09, 25.23, and $25.14~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}}$ (Figure 3c), and well match the $J{-}V$ curve values, which provide justification that the cell has almost no edge effect. In general, the primary consequence of optical loss in solar cells is the loss in current density $(J_{\mathrm{loss}})$ . The reflectance spectra of the PSCs are then measured (Figure S9, Supporting Information) and reflection-induced $\boldsymbol{J_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ value is further quantified.[60] When comprehensively integrated from 300 to ${800}\mathrm{nm}$ for the primary light absorption region of $\mathrm{FA}_{0.85}\mathrm{MA}_{0.15}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ perovskite, the calculated $\boldsymbol{J_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ values are 2.59, 2.42, and $2.52\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the asfabricated, HFCLP-, and HNCLP-contained PSCs, respectively. The HFCLP-modified device has less reflection-induced $\boldsymbol{J_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ , which is consistent with the EQE results. Compared to the control and HFCLP-modified PSCs, HNCLP-contained device exhibits the lowest leakage current (Figure 3d), also implying that the defects are effectively passivated by HNCLP molecule. Moreover, Figure S10, Supporting Information calculates the ideal factor (n) by measuring the photovoltaic voltage at different light intensities. The $n$ value gradually decreases from $1.823\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{T}/\mathrm{q}$ in the asfabricated cell to 1.504 and $1.217\mathrm{K_{B}T/q}$ for HFCLP- and HNCLPincorporated PSCs. The smaller the $n$ value, the fewer defects in the device, inferring that the HNCLP-contained device has fewer carrier capture sites and reduced trap-assisted nonradiative recombination.[61] Figure 3e shows the capacitance-voltage relationship curves (Mott–Schottky) for analyzing the built-in electric field $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ of the cells. The $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ value of the control cell is $1.11\mathrm{V},$ while the values of HFCLP- and HNCLP-contained PSCs jump to 1.13 and $1.17\mathrm{V},$ respectively. The high $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ raises the separation capacity of holes and electrons, accelerating the carrier transport process. The charge transfer and recombination process in all cells are studied by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) (Figure 3f), and the equivalent circuit model and the extracted parameters are shown in Figure S11 and Table S2, Supporting Information, respectively. Compared to the control and HFCLP-treated PSCs, the HNCLP-contained cell holds smaller series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ and transport resistance $(R_{\mathrm{tr}})$ , further confirming that HNCLP with electron-donating group has the ability to effectively passivate defects, accelerate carrier transport, and reduce carrier recombination probabilities. The trap density $(N_{\mathrm{trap}})$ of electron-dominated device is accurately quantified by the space charge limiting current (SCLC) approach (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The trap-filling limit voltages $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ are 0.66, 0.39, and $0.26\mathrm{V}$ for the as-fabricated, HFCLP-, and HNCLPcontained devices, respectively. Correspondingly, the $N_{\mathrm{trap}}$ values are $2.73\times10^{16}$ , $1.56\times10^{16}$ , and $1.17\times10^{16}\mathrm{{cm}^{-3}}$ , demonstrating that HNCLP can dominate the production of electron-type defects and thus reduce the energy loss during carrier transport.[62] The control, HFCLP-, and HNCLP-modified devices achieve stable PCE of $21.85\%$ , $22.97\%$ , and $23.99\%$ , and stable current densities of 22.75, 23.44, and $23.52\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ (Figure S13, Supporting Information), respectively.
Based on the above characterization of the PSCs, the importance of the molecules with push-pull electron structure is confirmed. To deeply reveal the effect of push-pull electron structure on carrier transport and recombination, steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved PL (TRPL) based on the samples on non-conductive glass are successively tested. The steady-state PL results present that the peak position of all samples remain unchanged (Figure 4a), but the intensity of the modified samples remarkably elevates and presents the order of $\mathrm{\DeltaHNCLP>HF}$ - $\mathrm{CLP>}$ control, stating that HNCLP effectively reduces the density of defect states and suppresses nonradiative recombination. The TRPL curve is fitted using a double exponential decay equation (Figure 4b). The $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ of the film modified by HNCLP (512.9 ns) is much longer than that of HFCLP-contained (371.4 ns) and the control (102.5 ns) samples (Table S3, Supporting Information). The root reason of the long $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ is the redistribution of the electron density and the increase of the dipole moment of HNCLP, which passivates the perovskite defects, resulting in a reduced quenching probability and a longer diffusion length of the photogenerated carriers.[63–65] To clarify the relaxation process of photogenerated carriers, transient photovoltage (TPV) measurement is performed (Figure S14, Supporting Information). The TPV curve indicates that HNCLP modified device has the slowest photovoltage decay lifetime, which also proves that HNCLP effectively suppresses carrier recombination and passivates defect. In addition, fluence-dependent PL measurements are performed to understand the recombination regime taking place in the samples. It is found that the PL intensity of all perovskite films increases with increasing laser excitation density (Figure S15, Supporting Information). However, the normalized spectral shape has hardly changed, indicating that the illumination source does not induce phase separation of materials within the observed timescale.[66] The PL intensity $(I_{\mathrm{PL}})$ as a function of the excitation density $(P)$ is explored according to the equation $I_{\mathrm{PL}}$ $=\boldsymbol{P}~\times~e^{k},^{[67]}$ where $k$ exponent directly relates to the recombination process. For the as-fabricated, HFCLP-, and HNCLPcontained perovskite films, the fitted $k$ values are 2.71, 2.18, and 2.09, respectively. When the $k$ is greater than unity, the recombination regime of band-to-band transitions includes the recombination of excitons and free carriers.[68] However, the near-bandedge optical spectra at room temperature do not exhibit any excitonic effects.[69] Hence, the relaxation processes of photoexcited states are primarily governed by the recombination of free electrons and holes. Figure 4c shows the X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of the as-fabricated film and the perovskite samples containing two molecules with push-pull electron structure, respectively. All samples exhibit the (110) diffraction peak at $14.08^{\circ}$ with the full width at half-maximum (FWHM) order of control $>\mathrm{HFCLP}>\mathrm{HNCLP}.$ . In addition, the peak intensity of HNCLPmodified film is greater than that of HFCLP-contained and the as-fabricated films, suggesting that HNCLP-modified film has better crystallinity. This is also reflected in the appearance of the films observed through scanning electron microscope (SEM) in Figure 4d, and the histogram of the grain distribution is drawn in Figure 4e. The grain distribution ${\approx}0.75~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in the control film is disordered and uneven. The grain size of HFCLP-treated film has been improved to $0.79\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , but the distribution is still uneven. However, the grain size of HNCLP-incorporated film is further enlarged to $1.02\upmu\mathrm{m}$ along with uniform grain size distribution, demonstrating that HNCLP is helpful to realize perovskite absorber with large size and few grain boundaries. Meanwhile, the morphology of the films is further explored using atomic force microscopy (AFM) (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The roughness of the control, HFCLP-, and HNCLP-contained films are 38.6, 31.9, and $27.9\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, and the grain size and distribution are consistent with the SEM results. The above serialized characterization illustrates that HNCLP-added film has larger grains, higher density, and lower roughness, all of which contribute to the carrier extraction and FF improvement of PSCs.
Due to the large dipole moment and appropriate electron density distribution, the contact angle images exhibit that the hydrophobicity of HNCLP-contained sample $(84.8^{\circ})$ is superior to that of the HFCLP-added $(77.8^{\circ})$ and the control $(69.0^{\circ})$ films (Figure 5a), which also benefits the humidity stability of the samples. Subsequently, humidity stability is tested at a relative humidity of $80\%$ and a temperature of $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 5b). In the control sample, the overall decomposition speed of the film is very fast. The sample edge appears yellow color after $40\mathrm{~h~}$ , and by $80\mathrm{~h~}$ , it has severely decomposed with the black color almost completely disappearing. The HFCLP-modified film only starts to decompose at the edge and shows the yellow color after $60\mathrm{{h}}$ After $80\mathrm{{h}}$ of exposure, although the black area in the center of the sample still exists, the yellow area on the edge slightly increases. However, the overall black color of the film modified by HNCLP has hardly changed. Despite sustained moisture erosion, a small yellow spot that appeared at $40\mathrm{{h}}$ has little change in the following duration. Simultaneously, the top-view SEM measurement reveals that the control film has been severely decomposed and the crystallinity is completely destroyed after $80\mathrm{h}$ (Figure 5c). Although the crystallinity of the HFCLP-added film is still maintained, there are large gaps between the grains, resulting in a decrease in compactness. Notably, the film containing HNCLP has almost no pores and still maintains good compactness.
The phase transition and decomposition states are simultaneously explored by XRD (Figure 5d). Initially, all samples present the $\alpha$ -phase along (110) diffraction peak. After $40\mathrm{~h~}$ under humidity conditions, the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak and $\delta$ -phase appear in the control and HFCLP-added films. However, only faint $\delta$ -phase peak appears in the HNCLP-contained film. As the test time goes by, the trend of decomposition of perovskite into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and phase transfer becomes increasingly intense, especially for the control and HFCLP-contained films. After $80\mathrm{~h~}$ of exposure, the peak intensity of black perovskite phase in the control and HFCLPcontaining films decreases precipitously, and the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and $\delta$ phase perovskite peaks rapidly increases and presents a dominant trend. However, within the identical time period, although the peak intensities of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and yellow perovskite phase in the HFCLP-modified sample are slightly enhanced, the $\alpha$ -phase perovskite peak still dominates. Finally, the air stability of the unencapsulated cells is compared and monitored at a temperature of $25\pm5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and a relative humidity of $30\pm5\%$ (Figure 5e). During the whole $576\mathrm{h}$ storage period, the PCE of as-fabricated PSC decreases continuously, ultimately maintaining only $76.5\%$ of its original PCE. For HFCLP-added device, the efficiency decreases rapidly within the first $25\mathrm{~h~}$ , then gradually deteriorates, and finally retains $83.9\%$ of its initial value. The HNCLP-contained device exhibits excellent stability for the first $400\mathrm{h}$ , then decreases slightly, and eventually loses about $6\%$ of its original efficiency.
## 3. Conclusion
Here, 5-chloro-2-hydroxypyridine derivatives of HFCLP and HNCLP are proposed to achieve push-pull electronic configuration, and the structure-activity relationship between their electron cloud distribution and the performance of perovskite absorber and PSCs is clarified. DFT simulation reveals that the functional groups directly contribute to the redistribution of the electron density of the derivatives. HNCLP with the electron-donating end group of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ owns a large dipole moment in comparison with HFCLP. The reasonable distribution of high-density electrons in HNCLP enables it highly effective targeted passivation for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and interstitial $\mathrm{I}^{3-}$ related defects simultaneously, which is further confirmed by experimental mechanism analysis. With these strong interactions, HNCLP assists perovskite grain growth and high-quality film, promotes the enhancement of the built-in potential and the downward shift of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{E}}_{\mathrm{F}}$ , and facilitates the extraction and transfer of carriers. The best-performance HNCLP-incorporated PSC has a PCE up to $24.47\%$ , surpassing $22.27\%$ of control device and $23.18\%$ of HFCLP-treated device. Meanwhile, HNCLP assists to enhancing the humidity resistance of perovskite absorber and device air-storage stability. The study presents a targeted strategy for the design of push-pull electronic structure additives applied in the photovoltaic field.
